881: ### Task 8: Scope the command-safety gate to command positions and unquoted syntax
882: 
883: **What:** Make the `git` command gate scan only the shell syntax that can actually execute — not quoted strings and path operands — and close the `$(...)` hole it currently leaves open.
884: 
885: **Why:** `evaluateCommandSafety` runs `WRITE_OR_NETWORK_COMMAND_PATTERN` and the redirection check over the raw command string, including quoted spans and path operands. Confirmed outcomes on the current gate:
886: 
887: | Command                                  | Current verdict | Correct verdict | Why the gate is wrong                        |
888: | ---------------------------------------- | --------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
889: | `git log --oneline -- docs/rm.md`        | ❌ BLOCK        | ✅ allow        | `\brm\b` matched inside a *path operand*     |
890: | `git log --oneline -- src/cp/index.ts`   | ❌ BLOCK        | ✅ allow        | `\bcp\b` inside a path operand               |
891: | `git grep -n "export-default"`           | ❌ BLOCK        | ✅ allow        | `export-*` inside a *quoted pattern*         |
892: | `git log --grep="remove-item"`           | ❌ BLOCK        | ✅ allow        | cmdlet name inside a quoted pattern          |
893: | `git log --format="%h <%an>"`            | ❌ BLOCK        | ✅ allow        | `<` `>` are inert inside double quotes       |
894: | ``git log --grep='back`tick'``           | ❌ BLOCK        | ✅ allow        | backtick checked before quote state          |
895: | `git log --grep=$(whoami)`               | ✅ **allow**    | ❌ BLOCK        | nothing checks `$(` at all — a real hole     |
896: 
897: The whole-command scan is also redundant at command positions: segment 0 must already be `git` and every later pipeline segment must already be in `READ_ONLY_PIPE_COMMANDS`. The only place a write command can actually hide is inside a `{ ... }` script block passed to a filter cmdlet, plus a `$(...)` subexpression — and `$(...)` is **not** currently blocked at all, so removing the blanket scan without adding it would open a real hole.
898: 
899: **How:** PowerShell quoting decides which checks apply where:
900: - **single quotes** are fully literal — nothing inside can execute, so blank them for every scan;
901: - **double quotes** still expand `$(...)` and honour the backtick escape, but redirection operators inside them are inert text.
902: 
903: Blank quoted spans before each scan (with the right blanking per scan), scan `{ ... }` script-block bodies for write commands, block `$(` and `` ` `` outside single quotes, and keep the producer/pipe allow-lists as the command-position gate.
